Register moddeps for a linked-in module that is never auto-loaded.

mkbltnmlst.sh emits add_dep() in two places: for modules with load=yes,
and for dynamic modules with load=no. A module that is linked into the
binary *and* declares load=no falls through both, so nothing tells
load_module() what it is built on. Stock zsh never notices, because there
such a module stays a .so and the missing dependency surfaces as a clean
"undefined symbol" from the dynamic loader. With every module linked in,
the same `zmodload zsh/deltochar` instead runs the module's boot code with
zsh/zle absent and dereferences a null keymap.

The sibling loop's own grep pattern is no guide here: it ends in a space,
while a config.modules line ends right after `load=no` unless the module
also declares functions=, which neither zsh/deltochar nor zsh/zftp does.
Match the two fields separately instead.

--- a/Src/mkbltnmlst.sh
+++ b/Src/mkbltnmlst.sh
@@ -100,6 +100,28 @@
     fi
 done
 
+# Same, for a module that is linked in and never auto-loaded: zmodload can
+# still reach it, and load_module() has to boot what it depends on first.
+stat_mods="`grep ' link=static ' $CFMOD | grep ' load=no' | \
+          sed -e '/^#/d' -e 's/ .*/ /' -e 's/^name=/ /'`"
+
+for mod in $stat_mods; do
+    modfile="`grep '^name='$mod' ' $CFMOD | \
+              sed -e 's/^.* modfile=//' -e 's/ .*//'`"
+    if test "x$modfile" = x; then
+	echo >&2 "WARNING: no name for \`$mod' in $CFMOD (ignored)"
+	continue
+    fi
+    unset moddeps
+    . $srcdir/../$modfile
+    if test -n "$moddeps"; then
+        echo "/* linked-in module \`$mod', not auto-loaded */"
+        for dep in $moddeps; do
+          echo "  add_dep(\"$mod\", \"$dep\");"
+        done
+    fi
+done
+
 echo
 done_mods=" "
 for bin_mod in $bin_mods; do
